      </div>
      <p class="eng-fig__cap">Table 2. Designed guide sequence candidates for <i>SST2</i> knockout (Cycle 1).</p>

      <h3 id="engineering-chassis-cycle1-build">Build</h3>
      <p>
        The CRISPR-Cas9 components targeting <i>FAR1</i> were assembled via a three-part co-transformation strategy. The linearized pML104 vector, the guide fragment containing the sgRNA sequence, and the ~600 bp repair template (containing the upstream and downstream homology arms flanking the target site) were directly co-transformed into competent <i>S. cerevisiae</i> BY4741 cells, relying on endogenous yeast homologous recombination for in vivo plasmid assembly. Transformants were plated on SD-His-Ura selective medium. As shown in Figure 1, the negative control plate exhibited no colony growth (Figure 1A), while numerous single colonies appeared on the experimental plate (Figure 1B), indicating successful transformation.
      </p>
      <p>
        To screen for successful gene knockout, ten colonies were randomly picked and subjected to colony PCR using junction-spanning primers (Figure 2). All ten clones yielded the expected target band (Figure 2, lanes 1–10), suggesting the presence of the target locus. The candidate clones were further verified by Sanger sequencing to confirm the exact sequence at the <i>FAR1</i> locus (Figure 3). However, the sequencing chromatogram revealed that the target 4 bp sequence (highlighted in yellow) was still intact, confirming that the intended genomic deletion had not occurred in this cycle. These results indicated that while the transformation and PCR screening were successful, the in vivo assembly or homologous recombination efficiency was insufficient to achieve the <i>FAR1</i> knockout.
      </p>
