          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="eng-fig__cap">Table 4. Designed guide sequence for <i>SST2</i> knockout (Cycle 2).</p>

      <h3 id="engineering-chassis-cycle2-build">Build</h3>
      <p>
        Based on the optimized strategy, we pre-assembled the sgRNA in vitro using inverse PCR. The empty pML104 plasmid was used as the template, and primers containing the specific guide sequences were used to amplify the linearized pML104-sgRNA backbone. Agarose gel electrophoresis confirmed the successful linearization of both the <i>FAR1</i> and <i>SST2</i> targeting vectors at the expected size of 11258 bp (Figure 4).
      </p>
      <p>
        In parallel, the repair templates were constructed using fusion PCR. The upstream homology arm, the <i>URA3</i> selection marker, and the downstream homology arm were assembled, extending the homology arms to ~500 bp on each side. The resulting <i>FAR1</i> repair template (1146 bp) and <i>SST2</i> repair template (993 bp) were verified by gel electrophoresis (Figure 5).
      </p>
      <p>
        The knockout process was performed in a stepwise manner. First, the linearized pML104-sgRNA-<i>FAR1</i> and the <i>FAR1</i> repair template were co-transformed into BY4741 competent cells. Transformants grew successfully on SD-His-Ura selective plates (Figure 6A). Following colony PCR screening (Figure 8A), positive clones (F1–F6, F8, F9) were selected, and the pML104 plasmid was removed via 5-FOA counter-selection to obtain the Δ<i>FAR1</i> chassis.
      </p>
      <p>
        Using this Δ<i>FAR1</i> strain as the base, the linearized pML104-sgRNA-<i>SST2</i> and the <i>SST2</i> repair template were then co-transformed. Transformants were obtained (Figure 7), and colony PCR screening successfully identified the Δ<i>FAR1</i> Δ<i>SST2</i> double-knockout clones (S1, S5–S7, S15) (Figure 8B). Final verification by Sanger sequencing confirmed the exact 4 bp deletions at both target loci (TGTC in <i>SST2</i> and CACT in <i>FAR1</i>), both of which introduced frameshift mutations to disrupt gene function (Figure 9).           These results confirmed the successful construction of the plasmid-free Δ<i>FAR1</i> Δ<i>SST2</i> double-knockout strain.
      </p>
